WEBVTT

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:03.350
A regional mountain belt needs a regional explanation.

00:00:03.750 --> 00:00:08.290
At a continental subduction zone, magma adds crust while compression shortens

00:00:08.290 --> 00:00:10.190
it. Both can thicken the continent.

00:00:10.370 --> 00:00:13.750
In a continental collision, one buoyant continent is partly shoved beneath

00:00:13.750 --> 00:00:17.090
another, producing thrusts, folds, and deep crustal roots.

00:00:17.570 --> 00:00:19.490
Mantle upwelling provides another route:

00:00:19.490 --> 00:00:23.410
heating and replacement of dense lithosphere can lift a broad region.

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:27.940
The Andes illustrate subduction, magmatic additions, and shortening.

00:00:28.120 --> 00:00:31.560
The Himalaya and Tibetan Plateau record India's collision with Asia.

00:00:31.780 --> 00:00:36.800
The Alps also reflect continental collision, while western Canada combines shortening,

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:41.600
magmatism, and accreted pieces. East Africa shows the importance of heating

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:45.940
and mantle upwelling. Australia's eastern highlands are a useful caution:

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:49.840
the supplied text leaves the age and cause of uplift under

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:50.360
investigation.

00:00:51.310 --> 00:00:54.270
Mountain building and erosion compete from the beginning.

00:00:54.710 --> 00:00:57.570
If uplift outpaces removal, relief can grow.

00:00:57.850 --> 00:01:01.490
When erosion removes mass, the buoyant crustal root rebounds upward.

00:01:01.890 --> 00:01:05.890
That brings formerly deep metamorphic and intrusive rocks toward the surface

00:01:05.890 --> 00:01:09.850
while erosion uncovers them. Rebound does not preserve mountain height forever.

00:01:10.250 --> 00:01:13.450
Continued removal gradually reduces both the mountain and its root.

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:16.740
Follow the removed material into neighboring basins.

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:21.240
Deposited sediment adds weight, bends the crust downward, and creates room

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:25.440
for further accumulation. The mountain and its sedimentary basin are therefore

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:27.280
connected parts of one system.

00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:29.760
Erosion is not making rock disappear;

00:01:30.040 --> 00:01:32.740
it transfers material and changes the loading pattern.

00:01:33.410 --> 00:01:37.930
Western North America's elevation reflects thickened crust and, in some places,

00:01:38.070 --> 00:01:43.290
thin warm lithosphere. Farther east, cooler thicker lithosphere helps explain lower

00:01:43.290 --> 00:01:47.210
elevations toward the Mississippi. The Appalachians have lost much of their

00:01:47.210 --> 00:01:49.510
former crustal thickness through erosion.

00:01:49.910 --> 00:01:53.550
The textbook's approximate rule is six kilometers of added crust for

00:01:53.550 --> 00:01:57.590
one kilometer of elevation, illustrated by Phoenix and Flagstaff.

00:01:57.770 --> 00:02:00.970
Treat it as a rule of thumb, not a universal conversion.
